WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:03.960
if there's one skincare&nbsp;
ingredient that works it's retinol

00:00:03.960 --> 00:00:08.880
pretty much everyone agrees - whatever skin&nbsp;
concern you have it's one of your best bets

00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:10.800
but is it all a scam?

00:00:10.800 --> 00:00:14.240
quite a few dermatologists and&nbsp;
scientists don't think retinol works

00:00:14.240 --> 00:00:17.320
"there is very little if any trustworthy&nbsp;
evidence available to support the use&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.320 --> 00:00:20.360
of retinol containing products to&nbsp;
improve the appearance of aged skin"

00:00:20.360 --> 00:00:21.920
"I think they are a total waste of time and money"

00:00:21.920 --> 00:00:23.400
and I can see where they're coming from

00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:28.640
when I started looking at skincare during my&nbsp;
medicinal chemistry PhD I saw a huge difference&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.640 --> 00:00:33.160
between what scientists are taught is good&nbsp;
evidence and what I was seeing for skincare

00:00:33.160 --> 00:00:37.720
but after 12 years of studying skincare science&nbsp;
learning about the cosmetic industry and getting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.720 --> 00:00:42.040
qualified as a cosmetic chemist I feel&nbsp;
like I can finally explain to you why&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.040 --> 00:00:45.960
skincare science looks so un-sciencey&nbsp;
and how to find products that work

00:00:45.960 --> 00:00:48.000
now some of you are probably thinking she's just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:51.280
defending this scam because she's&nbsp;
been sponsored by skincare companies

00:00:51.280 --> 00:00:55.680
but a lot of non-sponsored scientists and&nbsp;
dermatologists agree and once I break it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.680 --> 00:00:59.480
down I think a lot of things that you've&nbsp;
noticed about skincare will suddenly make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.480 --> 00:01:04.120
a lot more sense and you'll find it easier&nbsp;
to pick out evidence-based skincare products

00:01:04.120 --> 00:01:07.320
retinol is a retinoid these are ingredients based&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.320 --> 00:01:10.320
on vitamin A and they're&nbsp;
the superstars of skincare

00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:13.440
they work for lots of things&nbsp;
like acne pigment and wrinkles

00:01:13.440 --> 00:01:16.640
but not all of them have&nbsp;
the same level of evidence

00:01:16.640 --> 00:01:19.600
some of these are drugs they&nbsp;
have been extensively studied&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.600 --> 00:01:23.400
and that's where a lot of that great&nbsp;
reputation for retinoids comes from

00:01:23.400 --> 00:01:25.400
but what about retinol?

00:01:25.400 --> 00:01:29.480
PubMed is a database where most&nbsp;
peer-reviewed clinical trials are listed

00:01:29.480 --> 00:01:32.720
this is where products have actually&nbsp;
been tested on someone's skin and then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.720 --> 00:01:35.480
other scientists have looked over&nbsp;
the study before it got published

00:01:35.480 --> 00:01:40.440
if we do a really basic search for retinol&nbsp;
clinical trials it's not really looking great

00:01:40.440 --> 00:01:45.400
we've got 39 results and a lot of these&nbsp;
aren't actually on retinol on skin

00:01:45.400 --> 00:01:49.720
this is a pretty typical retinol study that you'll&nbsp;
see and it's actually one of the better ones

00:01:49.720 --> 00:01:52.840
we've got 40 subjects which is&nbsp;
pretty low by medical standards&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.840 --> 00:01:55.160
but actually really good by skincare standards

00:01:55.160 --> 00:01:57.680
it's placebo controlled which&nbsp;
means the skincare product was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.680 --> 00:01:59.840
compared with a cream that didn't have any actives

00:02:00.360 --> 00:02:04.040
it's double blind which means both the volunteers&nbsp;
and the people taking the measurements of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.040 --> 00:02:09.040
the skin didn't know which product was&nbsp;
which so they are hopefully less biased

00:02:09.040 --> 00:02:13.360
but you can see here we've got retinol it's&nbsp;
also combined with lactose and glycolic acid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.360 --> 00:02:17.920
so you don't actually know if it's the retinol&nbsp;
working or if it's one of the other ingredients

00:02:17.920 --> 00:02:21.120
most of the authors are also from&nbsp;
Johnson and Johnson which is the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.120 --> 00:02:25.120
company that makes the product so there&nbsp;
is a potential conflict of interest there

00:02:25.120 --> 00:02:27.840
and we haven't even opened&nbsp;
the paper yet so I'm sure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.840 --> 00:02:29.760
once we get in there there'll be even more issues

00:02:30.480 --> 00:02:35.560
so why does the evidence for the best&nbsp;
skincare ingredient look like this?

00:02:35.560 --> 00:02:37.920
well a lot of this is the government's fault

00:02:37.920 --> 00:02:38.840
thanks obama

00:02:38.840 --> 00:02:41.840
most countries draw a line&nbsp;
between drugs and cosmetics

00:02:41.840 --> 00:02:43.880
drugs are defined as products that can treat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.880 --> 00:02:47.400
disease or they can change the&nbsp;
structure or function of skin

00:02:47.400 --> 00:02:51.880
before an ingredient can become a drug&nbsp;
it has to go through an approval process

00:02:51.880 --> 00:02:54.800
this involves doing a whole bunch of&nbsp;
studies including on lots and lots&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.800 --> 00:02:57.720
of people to show that it is actually effective

00:02:57.720 --> 00:03:01.720
the company then takes all this data&nbsp;
and submits it to a regulatory agency

00:03:01.720 --> 00:03:05.920
they make sure there's enough evidence to&nbsp;
say that it works and it becomes a drug

00:03:05.920 --> 00:03:08.840
on the other hand cosmetics are&nbsp;
defined as products that have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.840 --> 00:03:12.480
superficial effects they just&nbsp;
change the appearance of skin

00:03:12.480 --> 00:03:17.560
retinol is classified as a cosmetic so this&nbsp;
kind of makes it seem like retinol doesn't work

00:03:17.560 --> 00:03:19.480
it feels a lot like this

00:03:19.480 --> 00:03:23.400
do you know what they call alternative&nbsp;
medicine that's been proved to work?

00:03:23.400 --> 00:03:24.280
medicine

00:03:24.280 --> 00:03:27.040
what this is missing and this is crucial

00:03:27.040 --> 00:03:30.840
a lot of the time whether something&nbsp;
becomes a drug is less about what it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.840 --> 00:03:34.800
does and doesn't do and more about other reasons

00:03:34.800 --> 00:03:40.000
this drug versus cosmetic definition was&nbsp;
created almost a 100 years ago before we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.000 --> 00:03:45.080
as a species realized bloody everything can&nbsp;
change the structure and function of skin

00:03:45.080 --> 00:03:49.720
at least in little ways including water&nbsp;
and even just a piece of plastic tape

00:03:49.720 --> 00:03:52.720
I mean look at these effects it&nbsp;
is pretty intense for something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.720 --> 00:03:56.640
that literally just sits on top of&nbsp;
your skin and doesn't even go in

00:03:56.640 --> 00:04:00.240
and some ingredients are drugs in one&nbsp;
country and cosmetics in other countries

00:04:00.240 --> 00:04:02.200
it's not like you can tell a molecule what it can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.200 --> 00:04:05.360
and can't legally do to your skin&nbsp;
after you've crossed the border

00:04:05.360 --> 00:04:06.040
you just can't

00:04:06.040 --> 00:04:06.920
they won't hear you

00:04:06.920 --> 00:04:08.280
they don't have ears

00:04:08.280 --> 00:04:10.720
so this is a common misconception

00:04:10.720 --> 00:04:15.240
how cosmetics are legally defined doesn't match&nbsp;
what cosmetics are actually doing to your skin

00:04:15.240 --> 00:04:18.560
but at the same time it would&nbsp;
be ridiculous and unnecessary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.560 --> 00:04:21.160
to regulate water and sticky tape as drugs

00:04:21.160 --> 00:04:24.520
the way it mostly works in practice&nbsp;
is that cosmetics aren't meant to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.520 --> 00:04:27.840
claim that they can change the&nbsp;
structure or function of skin

00:04:27.840 --> 00:04:29.920
and if cosmetic companies start selling really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.920 --> 00:04:33.560
potent and potentially dangerous&nbsp;
stuff then the government steps in

00:04:33.560 --> 00:04:35.440
but with retinoids you might be wondering

00:04:35.440 --> 00:04:38.120
they all kind of do the same&nbsp;
thing they work in the same way

00:04:38.120 --> 00:04:40.680
but some of them made it to the drug side

00:04:40.680 --> 00:04:42.400
why is retinol over here?

00:04:42.400 --> 00:04:46.000
this is a really good question and it's&nbsp;
almost entirely a historical accident

00:04:46.000 --> 00:04:50.160
here's a dermatologist who was involved in&nbsp;
the original research on retinoids for skin

00:04:50.160 --> 00:04:55.240
how did it come about that retinol is over&nbsp;
the counter and retinoids are prescription

00:04:55.240 --> 00:04:59.560
the reason is retinol has been in in&nbsp;
a lot of products including cosmetics&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.560 --> 00:05:02.560
for a long time for its antioxidant properties

00:05:02.560 --> 00:05:08.480
so it was already in over the counter products&nbsp;
and then as as it became apparent and the work&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.480 --> 00:05:14.080
of Sewon Kang and John Voorhees where they&nbsp;
showed retinol was a prodrug of retinoic acid

00:05:14.080 --> 00:05:19.200
once we became aware of all that then obviously&nbsp;
retinol might have some some benefit and it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.200 --> 00:05:24.840
it clearly does there are many many good really&nbsp;
good studies that show that's definitely benefit

00:05:24.840 --> 00:05:29.960
so the FDA couldn't say well we can't&nbsp;
allow this over the counter because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.960 --> 00:05:34.600
it's already there you know for another&nbsp;
reason just in a different concentration

00:05:34.600 --> 00:05:39.600
so there are some good reasons why&nbsp;
retinol isn't legally classified as a drug

00:05:39.600 --> 00:05:43.160
but if it works then where&nbsp;
are the scientific studies?

00:05:43.160 --> 00:05:45.920
here's the thing with the&nbsp;
peer-reviewed scientific literature

00:05:45.920 --> 00:05:49.120
not everything has an equal&nbsp;
chance of ending up in here

00:05:49.120 --> 00:05:52.464
there are a lot of good reasons&nbsp;
why we don't see high quality peer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.464 --> 00:05:54.880
reviewed clinical trials on cosmetics that often

00:05:54.880 --> 00:05:58.880
yes one of these reasons is that a lot&nbsp;
of stuff probably doesn't work that well

00:05:58.880 --> 00:06:01.920
but a really important reason&nbsp;
that gets missed a lot is

00:06:01.920 --> 00:06:05.560
there is just not that much incentive to do them

00:06:05.560 --> 00:06:08.200
research costs a lot especially clinical trials

00:06:08.200 --> 00:06:10.800
most peer-reviewed research is funded by taxes

00:06:10.800 --> 00:06:14.320
it's a lot easier to get funding&nbsp;
to research heart disease than skin

00:06:14.320 --> 00:06:17.920
and within skin research it's a lot&nbsp;
easier to get money to study drugs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.920 --> 00:06:22.360
approved to treat medical conditions&nbsp;
than How Retinol Make Skin Look Nice

00:06:22.360 --> 00:06:26.160
that means more skin studies are funded&nbsp;
by ingredient and skincare companies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.160 --> 00:06:29.400
which isn't as nice because there&nbsp;
are potential conflicts of interest

00:06:30.120 --> 00:06:36.200
but even if the studies were 10,000% honest that&nbsp;
still changes the sorts of studies that get funded

00:06:36.200 --> 00:06:41.120
as someone who owns skin I would love more&nbsp;
high quality clinical trials on skincare

00:06:41.120 --> 00:06:42.760
it's nice simple direct evidence

00:06:42.760 --> 00:06:46.400
you literally put the thing on a&nbsp;
bunch of faces and see what happens

00:06:46.400 --> 00:06:48.360
but that's not how companies think

00:06:48.360 --> 00:06:49.920
they don't have skin

00:06:49.920 --> 00:06:52.760
back to the government

00:06:52.760 --> 00:06:55.280
companies are going to do less trials on cosmetics

00:06:55.280 --> 00:07:00.160
clinical trials are actually needed before&nbsp;
you sell a drug but cosmetics don't need them

00:07:00.160 --> 00:07:03.000
if a company doesn't need to pay&nbsp;
for an expensive thing they are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.000 --> 00:07:05.560
probably not going to pay for that expensive thing

00:07:05.560 --> 00:07:09.720
and it is expensive back when I had&nbsp;
less back pain a proper clinical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.720 --> 00:07:15.000
trial for a dermatology drug cost $1.5&nbsp;
million (not adjusted for inflation)

00:07:15.000 --> 00:07:19.000
that drug versus cosmetic definition means&nbsp;
that even if a cosmetic "did something"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.000 --> 00:07:22.640
in a clinical trial the company&nbsp;
can't just slap that on a billboard

00:07:22.640 --> 00:07:24.800
so it isn't exactly the most guaranteed return on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.800 --> 00:07:28.360
investment compared to everything else&nbsp;
a company could spend their money on

00:07:28.360 --> 00:07:31.720
let's say a company has decided&nbsp;
to pay for a clinical trial and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.720 --> 00:07:34.120
they often do despite all of these barriers

00:07:34.120 --> 00:07:38.520
well-designed experiments are a great way&nbsp;
to test what works and make better products

00:07:38.520 --> 00:07:40.440
yay scientific method

00:07:40.440 --> 00:07:44.120
but publishing a study in a peer-&nbsp;
reviewed journal is a choice

00:07:44.120 --> 00:07:48.200
the company could also just NOT publish it&nbsp;
and just use the findings to improve their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.200 --> 00:07:51.600
own products instead of giving&nbsp;
free info to their competitors

00:07:51.600 --> 00:07:53.720
and this happens a LOT

00:07:53.720 --> 00:07:57.720
I've consulted for some companies who&nbsp;
invest a lot in R&amp;D and it is extremely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.720 --> 00:08:04.200
obvious that what we actually see is a tiny&nbsp;
sliver of the actual research they've done

00:08:04.200 --> 00:08:07.720
now for academic scientists doing&nbsp;
good research and not publishing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.720 --> 00:08:10.480
it in a peer-reviewed journal is super weird

00:08:10.480 --> 00:08:13.560
I know because academia is where I was made

00:08:13.560 --> 00:08:16.760
and it's because the incentives&nbsp;
are so different there

00:08:16.760 --> 00:08:20.920
part of why academics publish papers is&nbsp;
because it contributes to scientific knowledge

00:08:20.920 --> 00:08:25.640
it's just "how science is done" and that idea&nbsp;
contributes to a lot of Industry publishing too

00:08:25.640 --> 00:08:30.400
but a very big part of it for academics&nbsp;
is that's how you keep your job

00:08:30.400 --> 00:08:34.800
it's "publish or perish" - your peer-reviewed&nbsp;
publications are really important when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.800 --> 00:08:40.440
universities decide who to hire and who to&nbsp;
promote and when research funding gets decided

00:08:40.440 --> 00:08:43.920
so the peer-reviewed studies that do&nbsp;
come out are going to lean towards&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.920 --> 00:08:46.800
maximum benefit to the company at a minimal cost

00:08:46.800 --> 00:08:50.880
and a lot of the time this is just&nbsp;
the opposite of good study design

00:08:50.880 --> 00:08:53.400
for example a well-designed trial to test retinol&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.400 --> 00:08:57.000
would mean it's tested on its&nbsp;
own against a vehicle control

00:08:57.000 --> 00:09:00.000
that means you're testing a cream&nbsp;
with retinol against the exact same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.000 --> 00:09:03.720
cream without retinol to make&nbsp;
sure it is that retinol working

00:09:03.720 --> 00:09:07.320
if you're Big Cosmetic Company you've just&nbsp;
paid a whole bunch of money to show that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.320 --> 00:09:12.160
retinol and ingredient that every other&nbsp;
company can use in their products works

00:09:12.160 --> 00:09:16.600
on the other hand you can spend the same amount&nbsp;
of money and test your specific retinol formula&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.600 --> 00:09:22.360
against a plain cream or even against nothing just&nbsp;
to rig it a bit and make your product look better

00:09:22.360 --> 00:09:27.440
so if you're looking at it as purely an&nbsp;
investment it kind of leans very much one way

00:09:27.440 --> 00:09:31.520
"well designed" also usually means&nbsp;
more participants repetition is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.520 --> 00:09:34.400
really important in science the&nbsp;
more times you test something the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.400 --> 00:09:38.680
more confident you can be that your&nbsp;
result isn't just a one-off fluke

00:09:38.680 --> 00:09:42.000
in drug trials they test on&nbsp;
hundreds or thousands of people

00:09:42.000 --> 00:09:44.320
most cosmetic trials have less than 30 people

00:09:44.320 --> 00:09:46.880
why would you pay for 300 people when you could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.880 --> 00:09:50.480
just pay for 30 and still be&nbsp;
doing more than everyone else?

00:09:50.480 --> 00:09:54.200
so there are a lot of reasons&nbsp;
why a cosmetic company probably&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.200 --> 00:09:57.280
isn't going to pay big bucks for&nbsp;
a well-designed clinical trial on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.280 --> 00:10:01.840
retinol that they'll publish in a peer&nbsp;
review journal that ends up on PubMed

00:10:01.840 --> 00:10:05.800
the only real incentive would be to get&nbsp;
new customers who are nerdy enough to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.800 --> 00:10:10.840
impressed by a well-designed clinical trial&nbsp;
but they don't already think retinol works

00:10:10.840 --> 00:10:16.720
this is a really really tiny group of&nbsp;
people again just an awful investment

00:10:16.720 --> 00:10:20.640
especially since pretty much everyone&nbsp;
who understands retinoid pharmacology&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.640 --> 00:10:24.080
accepts that at least some retinol products work

00:10:24.080 --> 00:10:25.960
so why?

00:10:25.960 --> 00:10:28.840
why do so many scientists think retinol works even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.840 --> 00:10:32.120
though we don't have that many&nbsp;
high quality clinical trials?

00:10:32.120 --> 00:10:35.160
even kligman (boo hiss) who&nbsp;
invented tretinoin and has a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.160 --> 00:10:39.080
reputation for maybe hyping&nbsp;
it up a little bit too much

00:10:39.080 --> 00:10:43.760
even he says retinol which is&nbsp;
a competing ingredient works

00:10:43.760 --> 00:10:46.640
I love clinical trials I think all scientists do

00:10:46.640 --> 00:10:49.640
thing go on face face change nice

00:10:49.640 --> 00:10:50.120
nice

00:10:50.120 --> 00:10:53.440
but I think sometimes we forget that&nbsp;
the whole point of of science is to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:53.440 --> 00:10:58.320
build an understanding of the world so&nbsp;
we don't have to directly test everything

00:10:58.320 --> 00:11:00.320
clinical trials have been&nbsp;
important for such a long&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.320 --> 00:11:03.320
time because human biology is really complicated

00:11:03.320 --> 00:11:07.960
clinical trials aren't really used for better&nbsp;
understood areas of science like astrophysics

00:11:07.960 --> 00:11:11.200
it can be hard to accurately predict&nbsp;
what happens when you take a pill

00:11:11.200 --> 00:11:15.000
the ingredients have to travel through your&nbsp;
digestive system get absorbed go into your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:15.000 --> 00:11:20.040
blood get processed by your liver and then end&nbsp;
up in the right part of your body before it works

00:11:20.040 --> 00:11:21.800
but skin products are less complex

00:11:21.800 --> 00:11:25.800
they just have to go through a relatively&nbsp;
short distance through your skin so it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:25.800 --> 00:11:29.680
is a lot easier to work out what's&nbsp;
going on from other types of evidence

00:11:29.680 --> 00:11:33.200
and our scientific understanding&nbsp;
of human biology is also improving

00:11:33.200 --> 00:11:35.880
for example in toxicology&nbsp;
there's been a lot more use&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:35.880 --> 00:11:40.000
of new approach methods instead of just&nbsp;
directly testing on animals or people

00:11:40.000 --> 00:11:44.320
and this sort of scientific reasoning and&nbsp;
extrapolation is used in clinical trials too

00:11:44.320 --> 00:11:48.400
if a drug works in a clinical trial in one&nbsp;
place we assume that it's going to work in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:48.400 --> 00:11:52.880
lots of other people in lots of other&nbsp;
places maybe years after the original

00:11:52.880 --> 00:11:57.320
and that's because thanks to science we know&nbsp;
that human biology doesn't evolve that quickly

00:11:57.320 --> 00:12:00.440
drug molecules aren't really&nbsp;
affected by where you are

00:12:00.440 --> 00:12:04.240
clinical trials for tretinoin didn't tell people&nbsp;
whether they should be sitting or standing while&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:04.240 --> 00:12:09.360
applying it because again thanks to science we&nbsp;
know that's not going to change how it works

00:12:09.360 --> 00:12:13.040
so there's actually a lot of other&nbsp;
scientific evidence on retinol and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:13.040 --> 00:12:16.760
when you put it all together it builds&nbsp;
a really convincing case that if we had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:16.760 --> 00:12:21.800
the incentive to do high quality clinical&nbsp;
trials they would find that retinol works

00:12:21.800 --> 00:12:25.480
one of the key pieces of evidence&nbsp;
is that in your skin retinol can be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:25.480 --> 00:12:27.960
converted to tretinoin which is an approved drug

00:12:27.960 --> 00:12:30.040
there's a lot of evidence that tretinoin works and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:30.040 --> 00:12:34.800
we also know a lot about how it works&nbsp;
its pharmacological mechanism of action

00:12:34.800 --> 00:12:39.160
so far this isn't a slam dunk we still&nbsp;
need that tretinoin that gets formed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:39.160 --> 00:12:44.160
from the retinol to end up in the right&nbsp;
place and at a high enough concentration

00:12:44.160 --> 00:12:46.400
luckily we do have evidence for this

00:12:46.400 --> 00:12:48.960
we also have evidence that applying normal amounts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:48.960 --> 00:12:53.640
of retinol to skin will change the&nbsp;
